cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  proc-posets: a poset, cospan and string-diagram core for process mining
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite the archived release (the DOI below),
  not the repository URL. A URL moves; a DOI does not.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Antony R.
    family-names: Lee
    email: antony.lee@gmail.com
    affiliation: University of Birmingham
repository-code: 'https://github.com/AntonyRLee/proc-posets'
abstract: >-
  Reusable poset / cospan / string-diagram calculation, simulation, estimation
  and comparison core for process-mining research. It provides the canonical
  modular (block) decomposition of a labelled poset, a stochastic matrix
  distance and its known-law estimators, and screens that ask of a log
  whether it carries within-case concurrency or loops at all. The core is
  stdlib-only and installs at Python 3.10; numpy, graph, pm4py and plotting
  layers sit behind extras.
keywords:
  - process mining
  - partial orders
  - string diagrams
  - cospans
  - concurrency
license: MIT
version: 0.1.0
date-released: '2026-09-14'
# doi: recorded here once the release is archived.  Use the CONCEPT DOI -- the
# one that always resolves to the latest version -- so this file does not go
# stale at the next release.
references:
  - type: conference-paper
    title: Object-Centric Causal Nets
    authors:
      - given-names: Lukas
        family-names: Liss
      - given-names: Caspar
        family-names: Mensing
      - given-names: Wil M. P.
        name-particle: van der
        family-names: Aalst
    collection-title: Advanced Information Systems Engineering (CAiSE 2025)
    publisher:
      name: Springer Nature Switzerland
    year: 2025
    start: 94
    end: 110
    doi: 10.1007/978-3-031-94571-7_6
    notes: >-
      `procposets.occn` implements this paper's published algorithm (the
      Listing 1.1 pseudocode) independently. No code from the authors'
      reference implementation is included or redistributed here.
